Verified by execution: both tests fail at their expect_err under the
fetch-only mutation, for the stated reason. A 40-char fake oid cannot
accidentally resolve, because gix treats even full-length hex as a
prefix lookup against the odb; a short prefix of a real commit does
resolve, so short-hash pins are accepted consistently. An unreachable
source keeps the fetch error and never mentions the pin. A corrupted
slot self-heals on re-sync. All three consumers collect per-member
results with no ? in the loop, so the new Err is one member's outcome
and nothing aborts.

Two findings, one taken and one recorded. The no-commits message could
lie for a HEAD naming a deleted branch while other branches held
commits — built and proved; the message now leads with what is known,
that HEAD does not resolve, and names both causes. And a force-pushed
pin still reports synced on a machine whose cache holds the bytes,
while a fresh machine says pin not found: content-addressed and
defensible, recorded as a doc note for someday.

Blob declarations carry no rev, so the lying-pin shape cannot exist
there, and blob sync fails loudly on a missing index.

